{"name":"Southern Belle","hex":"#a6d6c3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"southern belle","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Southern belle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/southern_belle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Southern belle","blocks":[{"text":"The \"southern belle\", or just \"belle,\" was a colloquialism for a wealthy white unmarried woman in the planter class, particularly in the Antebellum South (1815-1861). The belle became a popular archetype, and, alongside the southern lady, served as the female equivalent of the southern gentleman. The southern belle image was popularized in the decades following the Civil War and became a key fixture of Lost Cause narratives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Portrait_of_Sallie_Ward_by_George_Peter_Alexander_Healy%2C_1860.jpg/330px-Portrait_of_Sallie_Ward_by_George_Peter_Alexander_Healy%2C_1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_belle","wikidata":"Q478109"},{"title":"Southern Belle (KCS train)","blocks":[{"text":"The Southern Belle was a named passenger train service offered by Kansas City Southern Railway (KCS) from the 1940s through the 1960s, running between Kansas City, Missouri, and New Orleans, Louisiana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Southern_Belle_Kansas_City_Southern_Railroad_1941.JPG/330px-Southern_Belle_Kansas_City_Southern_Railroad_1941.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Belle_(KCS_train)","wikidata":"Q7569672"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Leslie-Howard-Gone-With-The-Wind-1.jpg/500px-Leslie-Howard-Gone-With-The-Wind-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu